## 18. Core Security Principles

1. Do not reverse-engineer or bypass company systems.
2. Use official APIs, approved views, or approved exports.
3. Keep AI models and sensitive data centralized.
4. Treat PDFs, images, database fields and tool output as untrusted data.
5. Do not rely on prompts as a security boundary.
6. Filter data before it reaches the model.
7. Enforce permissions in backend code and the data source.
8. Give the AI narrow, approved tools instead of unrestricted SQL.
9. Do not store long-lived secrets on employee computers.
10. Use company SSO and MFA.
11. Check device health and compliance.
12. Use short-lived, revocable sessions.
13. Minimize the amount of data returned.
14. Separate technical administration from business-data access.
15. Use anonymized data for developers.
16. Do not synchronize the full database to laptops.
17. Use a remote browser or VDI for highly sensitive work.
18. Log access and tool calls.
19. Require approval for exceptional or high-risk operations.
20. Test continuously with prompt-injection and authorization cases.

## 19. Final Recommended Design

```text
Employee device
  └── Thin chatbot client
          ↓
Company identity provider
  └── SSO, MFA, roles, device status
          ↓
Chatbot backend
  ├── User and device authorization
  ├── Permission-filtered RAG
  ├── Approved company-system API tools
  ├── Response filtering
  ├── Audit logging
  └── n8n orchestration
          ├── Ollama
          ├── OCR
          ├── Local vision model
          ├── Vector database
          └── ComfyUI
                  ↓
          Local image-generation model
```

**The most important principle:** the AI may help understand requests and generate answers, but it must never be the component that decides what a user is allowed to access.

Use the company's existing security system as the source of truth, keep the AI and data centralized, expose only narrow approved tools, and give employees only the minimum data necessary for their task.

> The safest design: the chatbot asks the company system for data through an approved, read-only API, and the company system decides what the employee is allowed to receive.
